diff -ruN '--exclude=*.OBJ' '--exclude=*.obj' '--exclude=*.LIB' '--exclude=*.lib' '--exclude=BC31' '--exclude=OBJ' '--exclude=WOBJ' '--exclude=WATCOM' PCBSRCV/019/LIB/H/VMDATA.H.bak PCBSRC/LIB/H/VMDATA.H.bak
--- PCBSRCV/019/LIB/H/VMDATA.H.bak	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ PCBSRC/LIB/H/VMDATA.H.bak	2026-07-30 14:45:33.062240572 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,179 @@
+/*!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!*/
+/* vmdata.h â€” minimal shim for the Clark VMData virtual-memory array API.    */
+/* Written by: hexadecimal, v0.031.                                          */
+/*                                                                           */
+/* The real Clark VMData library provided disk-backed growable arrays for    */
+/* PCBSM's user-index rebuilder (INDEX.C).  The library ships in CDC's       */
+/* internal \LIBS\VMDATA directory which isn't in the public 15.3 source     */
+/* release.                                                                  */
+/*                                                                           */
+/* This shim implements the same API surface with a plain in-memory array    */
+/* using far heap allocation.  Adequate for indexing typical user counts     */
+/* (tens of thousands); a real disk-backed VM implementation would be needed */
+/* for a board with hundreds of thousands of users.                          */
+/*                                                                           */
+/* API required by PCBSM/SOURCE/INDEX.C:                                     */
+/*   VMDataSet   â€” opaque handle (struct)                                   */
+/*   VMInitRec(&set, NULL, 0, recSize) â€” init empty growable array          */
+/*   VMRecordCreate(&set, size, NULL, NULL) â€” append a new record, return   */
+/*                                              pointer to it               */
+/*   VMRecordGetByIndex(&set, idx, NULL) â€” get pointer to record N          */
+/*!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!*/
+
+#ifndef H_VMDATA
+#define H_VMDATA
+
+#include <alloc.h>
+#include <stddef.h>
+
+/* Opaque-ish handle used by INDEX.C â€” internals visible so it can be
+ * declared as a static VMDataSet Array; without needing pointer indirection.
+ */
+typedef struct VMDataSet_tag {
+    void far * data;         /* base pointer to first record */
+    long       count;        /* number of records currently stored */
+    long       capacity;     /* allocated slot count */
+    unsigned   recSize;      /* bytes per record */
+} VMDataSet;
+
+/* Initialize an empty set.  filename/type args ignored â€” real Clark VMData
+ * used these to spill to disk when memory ran low.  We keep everything in
+ * far heap.  Trailing args are the record size.
+ */
+static void VMInitRec(VMDataSet *set, void *ignored1, int ignored2,
+                      unsigned recSize)
+{
+    (void)ignored1;
+    (void)ignored2;
+    set->data     = NULL;
+    set->count    = 0;
+    set->capacity = 0;
+    set->recSize  = recSize;
+}
+
+/* Append a new record; return pointer to it.  Grows the array by doubling. */
+static void far * VMRecordCreate(VMDataSet *set, unsigned recSize,
+                                 void *ignored1, void *ignored2)
+{
+    (void)ignored1;
+    (void)ignored2;
+    if (set->count >= set->capacity) {
+        long newCap = set->capacity ? set->capacity * 2 : 256L;
+        long bytes = newCap * (long)recSize;
+        void far * newData = farmalloc((unsigned long)bytes);
+        if (newData == NULL) return NULL;
+        if (set->data != NULL) {
+            /* copy old data */
+            unsigned char far *src = (unsigned char far *) set->data;
+            unsigned char far *dst = (unsigned char far *) newData;
+            long i;
+            for (i = 0; i < set->count * (long)recSize; i++) dst[i] = src[i];
+            farfree(set->data);
+        }
+        set->data     = newData;
+        set->capacity = newCap;
+    }
+    {
+        unsigned char far *base = (unsigned char far *) set->data;
+        void far *slot = (void far *)(base + set->count * (long)set->recSize);
+        set->count++;
+        return slot;
+    }
+}
+
+/* Get pointer to the Nth record (0-indexed). */
+static void far * VMRecordGetByIndex(VMDataSet *set, long idx, void *ignored)
+{
+    (void)ignored;
+    if (idx < 0 || idx >= set->count) return NULL;
+    {
+        unsigned char far *base = (unsigned char far *) set->data;
+        return (void far *)(base + idx * (long)set->recSize);
+    }
+}
+
+#endif /* H_VMDATA */
+
+/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Additional API required by INDEX.C's createindexfiles():
+ *   VM_SHINT      â€” comparison-function return type (matches qsort's int)
+ *   VMSortFunc    â€” typedef for the underlying sort function (qsort-shaped)
+ *   VM_FALSE      â€” bool constant, used to select sort direction
+ *   VMSizeLock()  â€” real VMData locked the array before sorting.  No-op here.
+ *   VMSort()      â€” sort a range in-place using the supplied comparator.
+ *   VMDone()      â€” free the array's heap allocation.
+ * ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
+
+/* qsort-shaped comparator â€” Clark's VMSort passes the raw pointers so a
+ * comparator declared with `const void *` works.
+ */
+typedef int (*VMSortFunc)(void *base, size_t nel, size_t width,
+                           int (*compar)(const void *, const void *));
+
+/* Return type used by INDEX.C's comparator declaration. */
+#define VM_SHINT   int
+
+/* Sort-direction flags.  Real Clark VMData had VM_TRUE / VM_FALSE indicating
+ * some ordering option; INDEX.C only ever passes VM_FALSE.
+ */
+#define VM_FALSE   0
+#define VM_TRUE    1
+
+/* No-op â€” the real VMData would swap the array in from disk here. */
+static void VMSizeLock(VMDataSet *set) { (void)set; }
+
+/* Free the heap-allocated array. */
+static void VMDone(VMDataSet *set) {
+    if (set->data != NULL) farfree(set->data);
+    set->data = NULL;
+    set->count = 0;
+    set->capacity = 0;
+}
+
+/* In-place sort of records [start..end] using the supplied comparator.
+ * Signature matches the call site in INDEX.C:
+ *   VMSort(&Array, sizeof(IndexType2), 1, Highest+NumAliases, VM_FALSE,
+ *          comparenames, (VMSortFunc *)qsort, Buffer, sizeof(Buffer));
+ * We ignore start/end selection and just sort the whole thing â€” the caller
+ * uses this after loading everything.  Direction and sortFn args ignored
+ * (we always call qsort ourselves).
+ */
+static void VMSort(VMDataSet *set, unsigned recSize, long start, long end,
+                   int direction,
+                   int (*compar)(const void *, const void *),
+                   VMSortFunc *sortFn,
+                   void *scratchBuf, unsigned scratchLen)
+{
+    (void)start; (void)end; (void)direction; (void)sortFn;
+    (void)scratchBuf; (void)scratchLen;
+    if (set->data != NULL && set->count > 1) {
+        /* qsort() on the far-pointed data.  BC31 large-model qsort takes
+         * far pointer directly.
+         */
+        qsort(set->data, (size_t)set->count, (size_t)recSize, compar);
+    }
+}
+
+/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Additional stubs needed by INIT.C and SORT.C:
+ * ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
+
+/* Real Clark VMData would allocate the spill file and set up the swap
+ * subsystem here.  We're all-in-memory, so this is a no-op.
+ */
+static void VMDataStartUp(char *swapFile, int chunk1, int chunk2, int flag)
+{
+    (void)swapFile; (void)chunk1; (void)chunk2; (void)flag;
+}
+
+/* Real Clark VMData would register a callback that gets invoked when the
+ * VM subsystem is about to swap data to disk (for a progress display).
+ * With no swap, we never call the callback.
+ */
+typedef void (*VMSwapDisplayFunc)(char *msg);
+static void VMDataSwapDisplayFuncSet(VMSwapDisplayFunc fn) { (void)fn; }
+
+/* SORT.C calls this after a batch of VMSort operations â€” real Clark
+ * VMData would flush any dirty pages to disk.  No-op for us.
+ */
+static void VMSeqFinalPass(VMDataSet *set) { (void)set; }
diff -ruN '--exclude=*.OBJ' '--exclude=*.obj' '--exclude=*.LIB' '--exclude=*.lib' '--exclude=BC31' '--exclude=OBJ' '--exclude=WOBJ' '--exclude=WATCOM' PCBSRCV/019/LIB/H/WATCOMPAT.H PCBSRC/LIB/H/WATCOMPAT.H
--- PCBSRCV/019/LIB/H/WATCOMPAT.H	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ PCBSRC/LIB/H/WATCOMPAT.H	2026-08-05 17:57:11.754870384 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,292 @@
+/*!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!*/
+/* WATCOMPAT.H - Borland C++ â†’ OpenWatcom compatibility macros              */
+/* Allows PCBoard source to compile with both Borland and Watcom compilers.  */
+/* Written by: hexadecimal â€” pcbrevival project                             */
+/* Version: 1.1 â€” Phase 1.1 OpenWatcom port                                */
+/*!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!*/
+
+#ifndef H_WATCOMPAT
+#define H_WATCOMPAT
+
+#ifdef __WATCOMC__
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Memory model qualifiers â€” no-ops in Watcom flat model                  */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#ifndef far
+#define far
+#endif
+#ifndef near
+#define near
+#endif
+#ifndef huge
+#define huge
+#endif
+#undef _FAR_
+#define _FAR_
+#undef _FARDATA_
+#define _FARDATA_
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Far memory functions â†’ flat equivalents                                */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
+
+#define farmalloc(n)      malloc(n)
+#define farfree(p)        free(p)
+#define farrealloc(p,n)   realloc(p,n)
+#define farcalloc(n,s)    calloc(n,s)
+#define farcoreleft()     ((unsigned long)0x7FFFFFFFUL)
+
+#ifndef coreleft
+#define coreleft()        ((unsigned long)0x7FFFFFFFUL)
+#endif
+
+/* Far string/memory â€” same as near in flat model */
+#define _fmemcpy    memcpy
+#define _fmemset    memset
+#define _fmemcmp    memcmp
+#define _fmemmove   memmove
+#define _fstrcpy    strcpy
+#define _fstrlen    strlen
+#define _fstrcmp    strcmp
+#define _fstrcat    strcat
+#define _fstrchr    strchr
+#define _fstrrchr   strrchr
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* BIOS keyboard â€” bioskey() â†’ _bios_keybrd()                            */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#include <bios.h>
+#define bioskey(cmd)  _bios_keybrd(cmd)
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Disk/drive functions                                                   */
+/* Borland: 0=A,1=B,2=C. Watcom: 1=A,2=B,3=C. Adjust by 1.              */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#include <direct.h>
+#include <dos.h>
+
+static inline int getdisk(void) {
+    unsigned d;
+    _dos_getdrive(&d);
+    return (int)(d - 1);
+}
+static inline int setdisk(int d) {
+    unsigned total;
+    _dos_setdrive((unsigned)(d + 1), &total);
+    return (int)total;
+}
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* stpcpy â€” copies string, returns pointer to terminating null            */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+static inline char *stpcpy(char *dest, const char *src) {
+    while ((*dest = *src) != '\0') { dest++; src++; }
+    return dest;
+}
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* interrupt keyword                                                      */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#ifndef interrupt
+#define interrupt __interrupt
+#endif
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Calling convention                                                     */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#ifndef pascal
+#define pascal __pascal
+#endif
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Borland pragmas â€” suppress common warnings                             */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#pragma disable_message(202)  /* unreferenced parameter */
+#pragma disable_message(203)  /* unreferenced variable */
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Interrupt support â€” include i86.h for int86(), REGS, sound/nosound     */
+/* Watcom provides sound()/nosound()/delay() natively in i86.h            */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#include <i86.h>
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Pseudo-registers and geninterrupt                                      */
+/*                                                                        */
+/* NOT macro'd here â€” too many source files use _AX/_AL etc. as local     */
+/* variable names inside inline asm blocks. Defining them as macros would */
+/* break those files. The 2 files that use true Borland pseudo-registers  */
+/* (GIVEUP.C, MODEMFOS.C) are converted manually to use int86() + REGS.  */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* __emit__() â€” Borland inline byte emission                              */
+/* Used in EVALP.CPP only. Must be converted per-use to Watcom aux pragma.*/
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Borland dir.h constants â†’ Watcom limits.h                              */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#include <limits.h>
+#ifndef MAXPATH
+#define MAXPATH   _MAX_PATH
+#endif
+#ifndef MAXDIR
+#define MAXDIR    _MAX_DIR
+#endif
+#ifndef MAXDRIVE
+#define MAXDRIVE  _MAX_DRIVE
+#endif
+#ifndef MAXFILE
+#define MAXFILE   _MAX_FNAME
+#endif
+#ifndef MAXEXT
+#define MAXEXT    _MAX_EXT
+#endif
+
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/* Borland CRT odds and ends                                              */
+/*------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+#define setcbrk(x)    /* no-op on Watcom */
+#define getcbrk()     0
+
+/* unlink() â€” Watcom has it in io.h */
+#include <io.h>
+
+
+/* Borland _argv/_argc â†’ Watcom __argv/__argc */
+#define _argv  __argv
+#define _argc  __argc
+
+/* O_DENYNONE â€” Borland share mode constant */
+#include <share.h>
